{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman who served as the Count of Tripoli during the twelfth century. His life was defined by complex political alliances and military struggles within the Latin Kingdom of Jerusalem. He inherited his title at a young age after his father died in battle against Muslim forces. This early loss forced him to navigate dangerous waters while under the protection of powerful regents. The kingdom faced constant threats from Saladin and other Muslim leaders who sought to reclaim the Holy Land. Raymond often found himself caught between competing factions among the Christian nobility.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born around 1140 as the son of Raymond II of Tripoli. His father was assassinated by the Assassins sect which left the young boy as the ruler of a vulnerable county. The regency was managed by his mother and later by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. Raymond eventually assumed full control of his domains as he reached adulthood. He understood the importance of maintaining good relations with the Byzantine Empire and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His strategic mind helped him preserve Tripoli during turbulent times.\n\n*Political Alliances and Conflicts*\nThe count frequently clashed with other nobles over influence within the kingdom. He supported Baldwin IV despite the king suffering from leprosy. This decision angered Raymond of Antioch and other rivals who wanted more power. Raymond later faced accusations of treason when he allowed Saladin to pass through his territory. Some historians argue this was a pragmatic move to avoid immediate war while others see it as betrayal. The tension peaked when Raymond allied with the Ibelin family against the faction led by Guy of Lusignan.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy*\nRaymond died in 1187 shortly before the disastrous Battle of Hattin. His death removed a key stabilizing figure from the kingdom just as Saladin launched his final offensive. The loss of Tripoli soon followed after the battle as Muslim forces swept through the region. His legacy remains controversial among historians who debate his motives and actions during the crisis. Some view him as a cautious diplomat while others see him as a traitor to the Christian cause. The county of Tripoli continued to exist for another century before finally falling to the Mamluks. His story illustrates the fragile nature of the Crusader States and the difficult choices leaders faced.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0018163119}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.4305125e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0027763606}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.11009959}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.009909151}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.7151107}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.030371495}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.11617278}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.14282563}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -1.1935955}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -1.6689314e-06}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0039478284}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.00052543374}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.4305125e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.03099349}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.027732393}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.0072221453}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -3.9339143e-06}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -2.02656e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.06599406}, {"token": " His", "logprob": -0.6081618}, {"token": " life", "logprob": -0.006416178}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.2574894}, {"token": " defined", "logprob": -1.0838426}, {"token": " by", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " complex", "logprob": -0.195924}, {"token": " political", "logprob": -8.607281e-05}, {"token": " alliances", "logprob": -1.0307292}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.039512254}, {"token": " military", "logprob": -0.3443275}, {"token": " 